<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabernacle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mishkan, Tent of Meeting, Tent of the Congregation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, the Tabernacle was the portable dwelling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> until the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. Exodus 25–31 and 35–40 describe it: a Holy of Holies containing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a>, behind a veil, and an outer sanctuary with a lamp-stand, a table for showbread and an altar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>. Those who read the books of Kings literally hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple replaced it after 480 years in the Masoretic Text or 440 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>. Scholars compare it with tent shrines of Canaan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, among them a Midianite tent shrine at Timna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabernacle.jpg" alt="Tabernacle" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruk7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stiftshuette_Modell_Timnapark.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and Hymns for the Use of Jewish Children (1905), a closer one attributed to Frederick de Sola Mendes in The Jewish Encyclopedia (1906) and the Union Hymnal (1914), and two 21st-century versions that draw on these, in the Koren Sacks Siddur (2009) and the machzor <b>Mishkan</b> HaNefesh (2015).</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li></ul></section>
